\title{Standing policies}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{policy}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Rules the maintainer has given for RLVM and for this journal. When the maintainer gives a new lasting instruction, add it here in the same session, with the date. The maintainer's own words are kept verbatim, in the order they were given, in [the owner's notes](owner-notes), notes 1 to 16; the number at the end of an item is the note there. That document and the others of the repository call the maintainer the owner, so "owner note 12" in a tree means note 12 there. Order of authority for a design question: these policies, then [the reconciliation of the design review](design-reconciliation-document), then [the instruction-set document](design-isa-document) and [the architecture document](design-architecture-document), then the research documents.}

\subtree[standing-policies-goal]{
\title{What is optimised}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The goal is real checking performance: warm wall clock.} The optimisation target is the wall-clock time of checking with parallel workers in steady state, that is after warm-up (JIT compilation, table growth, the page faults of the first seconds) and with loading pipelined with checking. Warm-up cost is not charged: a JIT is judged by the steady-state speed it reaches, background compiler threads on spare cores are fine, promotion thresholds can be low, and the platform question (JVM with JIT against native image) is decided by warm wall clock. Instruction counts are the metric of the Lean Kernel Arena, not of this project, and are recorded for information only. CPU time is a diagnostic that tells how much parallelism is left. Correctness rules and the arena's interface (run line, exit codes, official verdicts) are unchanged by this. Given 2026-11-24; it replaces every "CPU time first" in earlier documents. Note 12. See [the decision](dec-7QMU) and [the measurement method](design-measurement-method).}
\li{\strong{Every benchmark reports in one order.} Warm wall clock with 8 workers (pipelined), warm wall clock with 1 worker, cold wall clock of a fresh process (for information), CPU time, peak RSS, instructions. Warm means that the same process checks the same export again after a first pass, with per-pass times reported, or the steady-state rate after the first seconds of a long corpus. Given 2026-11-24. Note 12.}
\li{\strong{The board's clock is not wall clock.} The arena ranks checkers by a virtual clock derived from instruction counts, and the leading position on that board does not reflect wall-clock time. A result is therefore never argued from the board's time alone. Given 2026-11-23. Note 1.}
\li{\strong{Do not chase instructions.} Prefer designs that are friendly to the processor's caches even when they execute more instructions: compact records, locality, fewer random probes into large tables, matching against a pattern instead of building and interning. Budgets and governors are expressed in time or steps, not in billed instructions. Given 2026-11-23 with CPU time as the primary metric; the metric itself was refined to warm wall clock on 2026-11-24 by the first item above. Note 10.}
\li{\strong{Realistic workloads are parallel.} Runtime and garbage-collector choices are evaluated with parallel workers and wall clock, never from single-threaded CPU totals. Parallel workers are the design point. Given 2026-11-23, in answer to a single-threaded collector comparison. Note 2.}
\li{\strong{Cedar-level workloads are the yardstick.} Heat and the economics of a JIT are judged on realistic project corpora where the kernel really reduces: the arena's cedar, con-leche, reachability, navier-stokes-euler and cslib corpora and Mathlib. Init and Std are not interesting enough to decide such a question. Given 2026-11-23. Note 9.}
}
}

\subtree[standing-policies-design]{
\title{What RLVM is}

\ul{
\li{\strong{RLVM is a mechanisation of the kernel's type checking.} The word is meant as in type theory: the worklist formulation of Zhao, Oliveira and Schrijvers (ICFP 2019) for higher-ranked polymorphic type inference, where judgments are worklist entries, inference judgments carry continuations, and the algorithm is a first-order small-step transition system. RLVM is the machine that steps such a system for the Lean kernel. It is not an abstract machine for the lambda calculus. Given 2026-11-23. Note 4. See [the machine](design-machine-worklist).}
\li{\strong{The Truffle language is the judgment language.} The guest language that Truffle compiles is the language of judgments and instructions of the mechanised check. Lambda terms and values are its data, not its programs. Given 2026-11-23. Note 5.}
\li{\strong{The work is an instruction set for normalisation by evaluation and definitional equality.} The design is stated as an instruction set; that document is linked in the first paragraph. Given 2026-11-23. Note 6.}
\li{\strong{The elaboration language decides how hot the machine is.} The language that constants and terms are elaborated into is the central design artifact, and the machine should carry essentially all of the checking work; the machine and its JIT are not to be confined to a corner of the workload by design. Given 2026-11-23. Note 3.}
\li{\strong{Natural numbers are specialised in the instruction set.} Small naturals are immediate values that never touch the value store; arithmetic and comparison instructions have a native fast path with an exact overflow guard that falls to the big-number path; the literal form and the constructor form are cheap views of each other; a recursor on a literal takes one step. Given 2026-11-23. Note 7.}
\li{\strong{Storage strategies are an open design question.} Specialised representations that hold while a value's contents allow them, in the manner of the array strategies of GraalJS, are to be explored with measurements and are not committed. A strategy that short-cuts a library definition is sound only if that definition is verified to have the expected shape, exactly like the prerequisites of the Nat and String literals. The value model leaves room for strategy tags and transitions. Given 2026-11-23. Note 8.}
}
}

\subtree[standing-policies-exactness]{
\title{What the default configuration must guarantee}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Never a false accept.} The default configuration never accepts a statement that is not derivable in Lean's type theory. This is absolute, and it is the first thing every review attacks. Given 2026-11-24. Note 13, item (a).}
\li{\strong{Official verdicts on every real input.} Everything the exporter writes (the corpora), the arena's good and bad tests, and the mutant corpora with their stored official verdicts get the verdict of the official checker. Everything the official checker accepts is accepted: a rejection is always confirmed by the reference engine, and a decline goes to it. Given 2026-11-24. Note 13, items (b) and (c).}
\li{\strong{Practical exactness, not strict.} Accepting a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own incompleteness, the non-transitivity of its comparison, its cache order or object identity is tolerated. The known classes are written down with one export each; see [accepted risks](accepted-risks). The machine may be the default engine once the first two items are shown by the gates and an adversarial review of the candidate has found no false accept. The two proof-comparison shortcuts are admissible; whether they are on by default is decided by warm wall clock over all corpora. Given 2026-11-24. Note 13, item (d). See [the decision](dec-TQSI) and [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). Both were carried out the same day: the machine is the default engine on that evidence, see [the decision](dec-T79V), and the shortcuts are on by default by that measurement, see [the decision on the default switch set](dec-YP0N).}
\li{\strong{Never a rejection of what the official checker accepts.} Tolerating an accept does not extend to the other direction: a rejection of a file that the official checker accepts is a wrong verdict also on a hand-made file. It is how item (c) of note 13 was read when such files were found on 2026-11-24, and what [the launcher's re-check under the strict policy](dec-7UAL) exists for.}
\li{\strong{The strict mode stays.} The reference engine is kept as close to the official implementation as it can be made, declining where it would need the official object identity. One launcher option selects the policy. Behaviour that exists only for bug-for-bug agreement on adversarial files belongs to the strict mode, not to the default. Given 2026-11-24. Note 13.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels as the official kernel compares them.} Both engines compare universe levels with the official kernel's incomplete algorithm by default; the complete relation is an opt-in switch only. The two arena corner cases \code{imax-right-successor} and \code{imax-nested-absorption} are rejected by default, as the official checker rejects them. Given 2026-11-24. Note 11. See [the decision](dec-P8XP).}
}
}

\subtree[standing-policies-conduct]{
\title{How the work is done}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No Oracle GraalVM by default.} The default build, the arena build and every default script neither download nor use Oracle GraalVM. It may be used for measurements that are reported to the maintainer and clearly labelled; nothing default depends on it. Given 2026-11-24. Note 11, relaxed by note 16 in the next item. See [the decision](dec-6E82).}
\li{\strong{One entry executable with the fastest default; the Oracle build with a shipped profile is optional.} The release is one executable, \code{rlvm}, whose behaviour without any option is the fastest configuration there is: engine, switch set, load mode, worker count, memory settings. A user does not choose between launchers or switch sets. A build with Oracle GraalVM and profile-guided optimisation, from a profile that is part of the repository so that the build needs no training, is an option of the build and never its default. The maintainer accepts Oracle GraalVM's licence on the development machine for experiments; the project has no commercial use of it. So: nothing default downloads or uses Oracle GraalVM; an explicitly requested build may. Given 2026-11-24. Note 16. See [packaging](design-packaging), [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG) and [the default switch set](dec-YP0N).}
\li{\strong{Continuous integration may use GraalVM Community Edition.} Automated builds may download and run GraalVM Community Edition, which is under the GPL version 2 with the Classpath exception. The item above is unchanged by this. Given 2026-11-24. Note 14.}
\li{\strong{Code is Apache-2.0 or MIT; the journal is GFDL.} The code of the repository is dual-licensed under the Apache License 2.0 or the MIT License, at the reader's option. This journal is documentation under the GNU Free Documentation License, version 1.3 or later; see the [licence](licence). Given 2026-11-24. Note 14.}
\li{\strong{Commit each step.} The history is needed: every step of the work is committed on branch \code{dev}. In the team of agents the orchestrator commits and the other agents do not run git. Given 2026-11-24. Note 11.}
\li{\strong{Up to 32 agents at a time.} The seats beyond the first sixteen go to profiling and optimisation. Given 2026-11-24. Note 11.}
\li{\strong{The journal is a Forester forest.} The notes of the project are kept as this forest, in the form of the Reussir agentic journal, and are prepared for publication as a web site. Given 2026-11-24.}
\li{\strong{Every note lives in the journal.} Design documents, decisions, research, measurements, reviews, working notes of a module, change logs and these policies are trees of this forest. Outside it only \code{README.md} files remain, one at the root and one per module or tool directory, with the licence and the instruction files for agents; a README says what a thing is and how to build, run and test it, and links here for everything else. The documents written before the rule, under \code{docs/} and beside the modules, are moved in whole once the work in flight is finished, and nothing new is added outside the journal meanwhile. The rule is recorded in the root \code{AGENTS.md} of the repository. Given 2026-11-24. Note 15.}
\li{\strong{No conversation references.} A tree may cite documents of the repository, commits and session trees, and never the content of a chat. Write for a reader who has no access to the conversation. Carried over from the Reussir agentic journal.}
\li{\strong{Identity is recorded.} Every tree names the agent that wrote it and the person who directed the work; every session leaves a session tree. Carried over from the Reussir agentic journal.}
}
}
